 fall football than spring  football  i'd say at this stage uh it's promising  you know we've um  it's amazing how much has happened in  the last five weeks since we made our  decision  and the good news is you know we got  access to the kind of testing our  medical  folks required sooner than we thought  we've gotten public health authority  approval uh uh you know quickly earlier  than we thought and so we've tried  we've tried to do a couple of things  then and i've appreciated the chance to  talk here we've tried to be transparent  about what we're doing deliberate health  and safety first  but at the same time our student  athletes want to play and our coach want  to play our fans want to play so  we've been committed to explore every  possibility to do so when  we felt it was safe and we had  appropriate approval so  we're trying to be nimble and flexible  too and uh uh you know our president's  chancellors have been that too and  that's why they're convening on a call  real time to address this new  information and yeah if we if we can  provide an opportunity for our student  athletes to play  as soon as it's safe they won't apply  and we owe them that  if we can do it you think you'll know by  monday if you're playing  i think we have to decide you know by  next week um  if we're moving forward do you have a  target date not yet  no i you know i need to this has been a  rapidly escalating series of events over  the last 24 hours  and um i need to see where my presence  of chancellors are which i'll know later  today but what  changed is it just the rapid testing  like  the approval of the governors of oregon  and california  for for us the game changer was  being the first conference to get access  to daily testing and those tests are  being shipped to us on monday  with the machines and the readers and  the reason for that  is our medical advisors you know that  concerns about heart  the prevalence of the spread of the  virus and  they uh didn't feel comfortable playing  the sport of football or basketball for  that matter  if they thought you know the practice or  the games could lead to the spread  of device beyond what people might get  in their communities  or at the dining halls or in their dorms  or whatever the act of playing football  or basketball couldn't lead to the  spread and  uh getting access to daily testing gives  a high degree of confidence that's not  gonna happen so that was the game  changer for us  and i think it was a game changer for  the public health officials  which was the second major thing that  just happened  uh you know within the last 24 36 hours  is it everybody has to play or nobody  plays in the pac-12  um up till now uh we've been completely  unified in our approach and i expect  that will continue to be the case  last time we talked and i i'm  paraphrasing uh  but it felt like you were saying that  the big 12 and the acc and scc were  being a little more reckless  you were being very overly cautious and  so was the big ten  do you still feel that way well i doubt  i ever used the word reckless uh with  with my peers  um they um i mean that's why i was  paraphrasing there but you  you were you were you sent a message  there whether you  intended to or not fair enough i think  what i was trying to suggest is we have  been more cautious  um and i think you know the values in  every part of the country  are a little bit different the  priorities  and the pressures that people feel and i  think our league and is something i'm  proud of with our schools  have wanted to air if they were going to  err on the side of safety  the health and safety and welfare of the  student athletes even if it would come  at a significant cost  tv revenue attendance uh  etc and people want to play um and we  drew the line in the sand we weren't  going to do it  unless our medical advisors were very  confident and comfortable  and of course we need public health  authority approvals and different  leagues m